1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,223
This presentation introduces Somama Siddiqui’s Senior Seminar project: comparing ways to keep a language model accurate when factual information changes.

2
00:00:09,223 --> 00:00:16,140
The project compares retrieval augmented generation, few shot prompting, and low rank adaptation, or LoRA.

3
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:23,979
The goal is to measure initial accuracy, errors caused by outdated knowledge, and recovery after an update.

4
00:00:23,979 --> 00:00:29,513
This walkthrough shows the implemented system and completed work through Week nine.

5
00:00:29,513 --> 00:00:34,540
The test domain is a synthetic cloud storage company called Nimbus.

6
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:36,825
Its handbook has two versions.

7
00:00:36,825 --> 00:00:41,852
Eleven evaluated facts include five stable facts and six changed facts.

8
00:00:41,852 --> 00:00:47,335
For example, the refund window changes from thirty to forty five days.

9
00:00:47,335 --> 00:00:50,534
The implementation also creates thirteen adaptation examples.

10
00:00:50,534 --> 00:00:55,561
Evaluation questions use different wording, but deliberately test the same facts.

11
00:00:55,561 --> 00:01:01,045
This measures factual learning and updating, rather than generalization to new facts.

12
00:01:01,045 --> 00:01:09,301
The repository contains two retrieval implementations, a few shot prompt builder, a shared evaluation pipeline, and CUDA LoRA training.

13
00:01:09,301 --> 00:01:13,211
Retrieval supplies a relevant handbook passage at inference time.

14
00:01:13,211 --> 00:01:16,687
Few shot prompting supplies question and answer examples.

15
00:01:16,687 --> 00:01:20,163
LoRA updates a small set of model parameters.

16
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:32,764
The Week eight and nine comparison uses the same Qwen two point five, three billion parameter model on a Tesla T four, keeping the earlier local Llama pilot separate.

17
00:01:32,764 --> 00:01:38,735
Here is an actual retrieval run using the repository’s T F I D F retriever.

18
00:01:38,735 --> 00:01:44,309
The question asks how many days after purchase a customer can request a refund.

19
00:01:44,309 --> 00:01:47,892
With version one, the retrieved section contains thirty days.

20
00:01:47,892 --> 00:01:52,669
Rebuilding retrieval with version two returns the updated forty five day policy.

21
00:01:52,669 --> 00:01:57,048
These passages were produced by running the code for this video.

22
00:01:57,048 --> 00:02:03,020
This demonstrates retrieval; the language model results shown next come from the saved GPU experiments.

23
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:12,538
The audited results show both retrieval methods answering ten of eleven questions correctly with version one, and all eleven after refreshing to version two.

24
00:02:12,538 --> 00:02:16,900
Few shot prompting scores ten of eleven on each matching version.

25
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,470
The version one LoRA adapter scores five of eleven.

26
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:23,643
Its version two update is not yet complete.

27
00:02:23,643 --> 00:02:29,592
The stale column re scores the original answers against changed truth, without generating new answers.

28
00:02:29,592 --> 00:02:32,764
These are measurements from one small, controlled pilot.

29
00:02:32,764 --> 00:02:37,407
One concrete failure appears in the saved version two few shot output.

30
00:02:37,407 --> 00:02:43,210
The expected refund window is forty five days, but the answer still says thirty days.

31
00:02:43,210 --> 00:02:49,014
Auditing individual answers also revealed that a stale answer can accidentally match a new policy.

32
00:02:49,014 --> 00:02:52,109
That is not evidence of learning an update.

33
00:02:52,109 --> 00:02:59,073
The project therefore retains raw outputs and separately audited scores, rather than relying only on an overall percentage.

34
00:02:59,073 --> 00:03:12,242
The saved training log confirms a completed version one LoRA run: thirteen examples, twenty epochs, and about one hundred twenty four seconds of training on a Tesla T four.

35
00:03:12,242 --> 00:03:17,237
Peak allocated CUDA memory was about three point three six gibibytes.

36
00:03:17,237 --> 00:03:20,870
This is framework allocation, not total device memory.

37
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:25,865
The repository preserves the training measurements, answers, scoring records, and report.

38
00:03:25,865 --> 00:03:30,861
Durable adapter backup still needs verification or recovery before continued training.

39
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:41,170
The main finding is that refreshing retrieval recovered all changed facts in this setup, while the small LoRA training recipe learned fewer facts initially.

40
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:51,051
These broad patterns have precedents in research; this project contributes its own controlled measurements and error analysis, not a proven new general discovery.

41
00:03:51,051 --> 00:03:53,628
Only eleven synthetic facts were evaluated.

42
00:03:53,628 --> 00:03:59,642
Week ten focuses on the version two LoRA update and retention of stable facts.

43
00:03:59,642 --> 00:04:03,508
Week eleven completes the comparison, limitations, and final report.
